london's latest attraction is almost  five meters tall and takes pride of  place here on trafalgar square  these visitors came from germany  especially for the unveiling  i love the matte color and when the  sun's out it really shines  the way the color glows is amazing it  looks great on trafalgar square  sphere  the square has traditionally been  dominated by nelson's column a tribute  to the british admiral who defeated  napoleon's fleet at the battle of  trafalgar in 1805.  the three other plints lining the square  likewise honor kings and generals the  blue cockrell is a lighthearted  challenge to the male grandeur around us  its creator is german artist katarina  fritsch  my main aim was for it to look good here  i wanted a sculpture that would be  interesting from all sides and would  work well with the historical  architecture which i love  but it wasn't meant to look  old-fashioned  katarina fritch is a major figure on the  german and global art scenes  she's known for her life-sized and  super-sized sculptures of people and  animals her trademark use of bold colors  lends her works an unreal dimension  it took the 57 year old two and a half  years to complete her latest work  it was a complicated it was really  complicated to create and make sure that  the chest wasn't too prominent it was  quite tough incorporating both the  cockerel's strut and its fragile aspects  this is the ninth work of contemporary  art to grace the fourth plinth on  trafalgar square previous exhibits  include mark wallinger's life-sized  figure of christ  a colored glass model hotel by german  artist thomas shutter and a very public  installation in 2009 by anthony gormley  where everyday londoners made their own  statements on the plinth  the plinth has also hosted a giant ship  in a bottle  and a bronze sculpture of a boy on a  rocking horse  now katarina fritch's cockrell has taken  up the perch  we as the fourth point commissioning  group ask artists to respond to their  location to think about the social the  cultural the political resonances of  this historic square in the center of  london tatarina fritz responded with  something that's simple that's elegant  that has a sense of humor and a real  beauty to it i think this will be a  piece of work that london will love  every work to feature on the fourth  plinth has sparked controversy and the  rooster is no exception  some see the cockerel a symbol of france  right next to nelson's column as a  provocation  but most agree it's definitely  eye-catching  i like it very much yes  it's  something new and modern  the blue color is great and a nice  contrast to this historic square  plus it's a very good representation of  a cockerel and it's nice and bright  and looks very good up there but i'm not  sure it's what i would want there as a  person as a permanent  position on the plinth  but for the next 18 months at least the  big bird will rule the roost here at  trafalgar square
 

